thread:
jules: Heads up — Acme Retail is seeing mid-stream 429s while streaming SSE, and their billing ledger shows duplicate token charges after reconnects. Full customer repro and logs below.

emma: ugh. can you paste a minimal curl repro?

jules: repro steps (customer environment):
1) start SSE request to /v1/stream with Authorization: Bearer <token-A> and header X-Resume-Session: true
2) after ~6s the connection is dropped by an intermediary (customer says their CDN reset)
3) client reconnects with same resume-id and Authorization rotates to token-B (short-lived token rotation)
4) server resumes + continues streaming but billing ledger shows both token-A and token-B charged for overlapping ranges, and customer sees duplicate partial outputs in the UI.

jules: curl snippet they used:```
curl -N \ 
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \ 
  -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \ 
  -H "X-Resume-Session: true" \ 
  'https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream?model=gpt-x&max_tokens=512'
```

raj: logs from the edge (sanitized):
```
[2024-08-01T18:02:15Z] conn=abcd123 start stream session resume_id=rz-91 token=tok-A
[2024-08-01T18:02:21Z] conn=abcd123 upstream reset by peer (cdn) code=RST
[2024-08-01T18:02:22Z] conn=efgh456 resume attempt resume_id=rz-91 token=tok-B
[2024-08-01T18:02:22Z] stream=rz-91 reopened route=serve-04
[2024-08-01T18:02:24Z] billing: incremented token-bucket for token-A range 0..340
[2024-08-01T18:02:26Z] billing: incremented token-bucket for token-B range 341..780
[2024-08-01T18:02:26Z] response: 429 mid-stream to conn=efgh456 -> client reconnects
```

emma: hypothesis: resume path doesn't atomically reassign ownership of unbilled tokens when the Authorization header changes mid-resume. we may be attributing pre-drop tokens to token-A and resumed tokens to token-B without collapsing ranges. if client reconnects quickly we might double-bill overlapping kv-cache ranges.

sophia: customer impact = duplicate charges + UI duplicates. they flagged potential overcharge of ~14% on a 2-hour job. need an audit and immediate mitigation.

ops-oncall: can we reproduce locally with a token-rotation script + simulated connection drop? if so we can create a hotfix to mark resumed-session ownership sticky to the initial resume token id for X seconds.

jules: repro script they ran (node fetch):```
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
let tokenA = 'tok-A';
let tokenB = 'tok-B';
let resumeId = 'rz-91';
(async () => {
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream?model=gpt-x', {
    headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${tokenA}`, 'Accept': 'text/event-stream', 'X-Resume-Session': resumeId }
  });
  // read 6s then simulate drop
  setTimeout(() => { r.body.destroy(); }, 6000);
  // reconnect after 1s with rotated token
  setTimeout(async () => {
    await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream?model=gpt-x', { headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${tokenB}`, 'Accept': 'text/event-stream', 'X-Resume-Session': resumeId } });
  }, 1000);
})();
```

emma: thanks — i'll try to reproduce on my laptop with a local proxy that drops connections.

deploy-bot: deploy queued: fix/stream-resume-lock -> staging (ci/6543)

raj: quick mitigation options for customers before a fix:
- advice: disable resume (X-Resume-Session: false) to avoid reconnect merge and accept fresh sessions (may increase latency/cost)
- advice: pin auth tokens for session lifetime (avoid rotation during streaming)
- server-side: add transient resume-owner cookie keyed to resume-id at first attach, ignore Authorization header changes for 30s

sophia: billing ask: create an audit report correlating resume-id rz-91, invoice ids, and token-ids tok-A/tok-B. customer requests a refund for duplicate range 0..340 if duplicate billing confirmed.

jules: requested logs + ledger dump from the customer (they uploaded correlation id: acme-2024-08-01-187). i flagged billing to start the audit.

emma: reproduced locally with the node script + proxy drop. confirmed double-counting when Authorization changed mid-resume. created a short patch to keep resume ownership sticky to first-token for the resume-id lifetime. running integration tests now.

ops-oncall: rolling a hotfix to staging for validation. if green we'll opt for a fast rollback-safe deploy.

sophia: looping in customer success to coordinate refund and messaging: @jules please draft a short note explaining root cause and mitigation steps. keep it technical but clear.

jules: will do. draft below: "We traced the issue to how resume sessions are attributed across token rotations. When a connection drops and the client reconnects with a different auth token, our resume path could attribute different token ranges separately leading to duplicate accounting. We're patching the resume-owner assignment to be sticky for short-lived resume sessions and will audit your ledger and refund any duplicated charges."

emma: test results: staging tests show no double-counting for 5000 simulated reconnects with token rotation. pushing canary now.

jules: customer reply: thanks, appreciate quick response — please share ETA on canary and any temporary headers we should set.

ops-oncall: ETA canary in ~30m, full rollout after 2h of canary metrics. temporary header: set X-Resume-Session: false if you cannot wait for patch.

jules: tagging resolved-for-now. will follow up once billing audit completes. :thumbsup:
